--- body ---
## Context

A reader unfamiliar with the ledger could not infer the distinction between
Entries and On the record from the labels or the phrase “read under the
ledger's schema.” The “Inside the portfolio” table on each Funding country
page is the first eight rows of the Projects table, without a ranking.
“Who is named, and where” currently keys rows by `(name, role, country)`, so
one name with both Funder and Operator roles appears twice; the Projects
column shows a count behind a fold-out rather than the names. These are three
parts of one task: each step of The paper trail should answer a different
reader question, in ordinary language, without overstating what the sources
establish.
The Entries detail currently escapes every non-term field as plain text, so
URLs embedded in `raw_project_description` of South Africa register rows
cannot be opened from the row.

## Proposed public copy for author review

Keep the six-step order. Proposed navigation labels: **Documents · Document
rows · Statements · Projects · Funding · Organisations**. Preserve the old
hash addresses as forwards if labels and canonical addresses change. Avoid
“raw,” “parsed,” “facts,” “evidence,” “ledger schema,” and “verified” in the
public explanation: rows are selected extractions, statements are attributed
and may be disputed, and neither is a count of unique projects.

| Page | Proposed title | Proposed opening paragraph |
|---|---|---|
| The paper trail | Follow a number back to its document | Start with a project, amount or count, then follow the links to the statement we recorded and the document page behind it. You can also start with a document and see what we drew from it. The steps below show different views of the material, so their row counts should not be added together. |
| Documents | Reports, plans and registers | See the publications we looked for, who issued them, when we retrieved them, and whether an archived copy is available. A listing documents a search or retrieval; it does not endorse everything the publication says. |
| Document rows (currently Entries) | Rows as the documents print them | Browse selected rows from registers, annexes and lists, with their original wording, columns and document pages. One row can describe a programme, a project, or something else; it is not automatically one unique project. |
| Statements (currently On the record) | What the documents say | Browse individual statements we recorded about amounts, dates, financing or implementation status, each attributed to its publisher and document page. Several statements may come from one document row, and some come from prose. A reported approval is not a payment. |
| Projects | Named projects, programmes and components | Search the undertakings named in the documents and follow each one to its statements and sources. Programmes and components can overlap; this is not a count of distinct physical assets. |
| Funding | Financing reported for each partnership | Compare each partnership's published headline with the financing statements recorded for it. Pledges, needs, approvals, signatures and payments are different milestones; amounts from different documents or milestones are not added here. |
| Organisations (currently Who's who) | Organisations named in the documents | See which organisations are named as funders or operators, the projects that name them, and any reviewed alternative names. A matching spelling alone is not proof that two organisations are the same. |

On a country page, use the country name plus the same plain-English purpose.
Replace “read under the ledger's schema” with “recorded as an amount, date or
status and linked to the document page.” Where the served data also includes
project-document links, name them explicitly instead of calling every item a
financial or implementation statement. The introduction and The paper trail,
Methods, README and country pages must agree. Show one concrete example of a
document row yielding a statement; state that there is no guaranteed 1:1
pairing. The author can adjust these exact words in review while preserving
the distinctions.

## Funding: a different table

Replace “Inside the portfolio” on each `#funding/<CODE>` page with **Financing
statements in the documents**, one row per *financial statement*, not one row
per project. Proposed columns: project or programme (linked); publisher's
milestone; amount and original currency (or “not stated”); funder and
instrument where stated; event date or reporting date, labelled by its actual
role; document/page. Keep the attributed country headline above the table.
Show needs separately from financing; a need is not money provided. Do not
sum rows, convert currencies, or treat repeated amounts at approval and
signature as two tranches. Preserve missingness and conflicting statements.
A visitor can filter by milestone and funder and open the supporting document.
The Projects page remains the complete, searchable list of named undertakings;
the Funding page links to it without repeating its first eight rows. Use the
current financial-event view until 0876 supplies ontology observations, then
use those observations without changing the reader-facing meaning.

## Organisations: one row per name and country

Combine exact same-name, same-country Funder and Operator rows into one row
with both role badges. Deduplicate linked projects by stable project ID. Keep
the role filter inclusive: a row with both roles appears under either filter.
When a reviewed party identity and its `party-names` forms exist, show its
preferred name and the recorded aliases with their provenance. Never merge
similar spellings or guessed acronyms into one organisation without the
reviewed identity decision (0875). Make aliases discoverable by hover *and*
keyboard focus/click; a tooltip alone is insufficient.

Replace the “Project · 1” or “Projects · N” collapsed cell with links to up
to three project names, then a labelled “and X more” disclosure showing the
remaining linked names. Counts, if shown, are secondary; keep source names
and countries legible at narrow width. The row should still link to each
project's detail page.

## The tallies: name the historical pool

Rename **Comparisons** in The tallies to **Non-JETP energy operations**. This
names the records more accurately than “Non-JETP projects”: the World Bank
source calls them operations, and administrative closing is not physical
project completion. Page title proposal: **Earlier energy operations outside
the JETPs**. Opening paragraph proposal: “Browse closed World Bank
energy-related operations approved before each country's JETP. They offer
historical context for financing timelines, not a comparison between JETP
countries or evidence that the partnerships caused a change.” Keep the
mixed-sector and additional-financing qualifications and links to official
records. Update the header menu, section sub-bar, page title, internal links,
README and presentation document together. Preserve `#comparisons` and its
earlier aliases as forwarding addresses, including the country filter.

## Entries: links embedded in document text

In the “Label and document columns” fold-out, render valid `http://` and
`https://` URLs inside `raw_project_description` as clickable external links.
Keep the surrounding description exactly as printed and keep the CSV/JSON
value unchanged. Treat the field as untrusted text: escape ordinary
characters and the destination attribute, accept only HTTP(S), trim
sentence-ending punctuation from the link, and use `target="_blank"
rel="noopener noreferrer"`. Handle more than one URL in a description.
An invalid or unsupported URL stays visible as text. Restrict this behavior
to fields intended to contain document text; do not turn every field value
into HTML. Include a browser check using an actual ZAF row such as ACTIP001
or CAN001, plus a malicious or malformed value that must remain inert.

## Relevant files

- `deliverables/jetp-observatory/app.js`, `styles.css` — page copy and tables
- `deliverables/jetp-observatory/README.md`
- `docs/jetp-observatory-presentation.md`, `docs/jetp-language.md`
- `data/jetp/parties.csv`, `party-names.csv` (reviewed aliases); 0875 adds funders and channels
- `tests/test_jetp_observatory_render.py`, `tests/browser/jetp_observatory.py`

## Exit criteria

- [ ] A cold reader can explain the difference between document rows and attributed statements from the page titles, opening paragraphs and one example; the public copy contains no internal schema jargon and does not equate rows with projects or approvals with payments.
- [ ] Funding's country table is financing-oriented and no longer invokes `projectTable(d.projects.slice(0, 8))`; each row has a source and date role, needs are separate, and no cross-stage or cross-currency sum appears.
- [ ] One same-name funder/operator within a country appears once with both roles and no duplicate project links; each role filter still finds it. Reviewed aliases are visible by mouse and keyboard without accepting unreviewed identity matches.
- [ ] Up to three project names are directly visible in each organisation row; the rest open through an “and X more” disclosure with working project links.
- [ ] The tallies labels the historical World Bank pool as non-JETP energy operations; no navigation text implies a country-to-country JETP comparison, and old addresses still forward with their filters.
- [ ] URLs embedded in an Entries `raw_project_description` open the intended HTTP(S) page; prose and punctuation remain readable, multiple URLs work, and unsafe text cannot become executable markup or a non-HTTP(S) link.
- [ ] Desktop and phone browser checks cover the copy, table width, filters, links and disclosures. Existing URLs forward if labels change. Run the checks required by AGENTS.md for a site/code change and record them on the PR.
